1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,756
Section 5.4 already gave you a naming pattern: brand, angle, version, placement.

2
00:00:04,756 --> 00:00:07,676
That answered a real question, which file goes where.

3
00:00:07,676 --> 00:00:12,302
It never answered a different one, which file actually won, and that second question

4
00:00:12,302 --> 00:00:14,891
is the one that gets expensive to answer badly.

5
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:19,297
Six months from now, someone, possibly you, is going to need to know whether the

6
00:00:19,297 --> 00:00:23,814
"hour eleven" angle has already been tested, what its result was, and whether it's

7
00:00:23,814 --> 00:00:25,026
worth another variant.

8
00:00:25,026 --> 00:00:28,992
If the answer to that lives only in someone's memory, it's already gone.

9
00:00:28,992 --> 00:00:32,186
Why does this matter more here than in most creative work?

10
00:00:32,186 --> 00:00:34,555
Because this course is built around volume.

11
00:00:34,555 --> 00:00:38,355
A brand running a handful of ads a year can rely on memory just fine.

12
00:00:38,355 --> 00:00:42,872
An account running eight ad sets a week, the way Section 9.1's sprint asks you to,

13
00:00:42,872 --> 00:00:47,498
generates more history in a month than memory can honestly hold onto by month three,

14
00:00:47,498 --> 00:00:48,600
let alone month six.

15
00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:53,605
Trying to cram a result into a filename is how you end up with names nobody can

16
00:00:53,605 --> 00:00:54,676
parse a year later.

17
00:00:54,676 --> 00:00:59,074
The filename's job stays exactly what Section 5.4 gave it: find the file fast.

18
00:00:59,074 --> 00:01:03,303
A separate, linked record does the second job: what happened when this ran.

19
00:01:03,303 --> 00:01:05,728
That record doesn't need to be complicated.

20
00:01:05,728 --> 00:01:10,126
A single spreadsheet row per ad set, linked by the same angle and version tags

21
00:01:10,126 --> 00:01:12,099
already in the filename, is enough.

22
00:01:12,099 --> 00:01:16,497
The naming convention and the results record are two halves of one system, not

23
00:01:16,497 --> 00:01:17,625
competing standards.

24
00:01:17,625 --> 00:01:21,121
Notice, too, why the link matters more than either half alone.

25
00:01:21,121 --> 00:01:25,858
A filename with no linked record tells you where a file is and nothing about whether

26
00:01:25,858 --> 00:01:27,324
it's worth touching again.

27
00:01:27,324 --> 00:01:31,947
A results record with no consistent filename convention tells you a winner existed

28
00:01:31,947 --> 00:01:34,879
once, with no reliable way back to the actual asset.

29
00:01:34,879 --> 00:01:36,909
Neither half does the job by itself.

30
00:01:37,459 --> 00:01:39,409
Here's the whole system, assembled.

31
00:01:39,409 --> 00:01:44,034
The filename answers "where is it." The record answers "did it work." Both keyed to

32
00:01:44,034 --> 00:01:48,603
the same angle and version tag, so anyone can move between them in seconds instead

33
00:01:48,603 --> 00:01:50,720
of reconstructing history from memory.

34
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:54,843
Save this exactly as it is, the same way Section 6.6 asked you to save the

35
00:01:54,843 --> 00:01:56,626
pre-flight checklist unmodified.

36
00:01:56,626 --> 00:01:59,524
The value isn't in the specific spreadsheet columns.

37
00:01:59,524 --> 00:02:04,092
It's in having one linked system at all, built before the volume makes it painful,

38
00:02:04,092 --> 00:02:04,650
not after.

39
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:09,274
Set this up before your next test, not after your fiftieth ad set makes the backlog

40
00:02:09,274 --> 00:02:10,667
feel too large to tackle.

41
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:15,347
Retrofitting a naming system onto months of untracked history is real work, hours of

42
00:02:15,347 --> 00:02:17,019
guessing which file was which.

43
00:02:17,019 --> 00:02:21,421
Building it into the habit from the start costs you almost nothing, a few extra

44
00:02:21,421 --> 00:02:24,429
seconds per ad set, paid the same day you generate it.

45
00:02:24,979 --> 00:02:29,034
One honest cost, easy to underweight because it doesn't bite for months.

46
00:02:29,034 --> 00:02:32,413
An untracked test doesn't just waste that one test's budget.

47
00:02:32,413 --> 00:02:35,623
It quietly disables a decision you'll need to make later.

48
00:02:35,623 --> 00:02:39,847
Section 8.4 asks whether to iterate on a tired winner or try something new.

49
00:02:39,847 --> 00:02:44,521
Answering that honestly requires knowing what's already been tried, and a memory is

50
00:02:44,521 --> 00:02:48,632
a much worse record than a spreadsheet nobody had to remember to consult.

51
00:02:48,632 --> 00:02:52,293
Think about the actual failure mode this produces if you skip it.

52
00:02:52,293 --> 00:02:57,024
Not a dramatic loss, just a slow one: the same near-identical angle quietly retested

53
00:02:57,024 --> 00:03:01,585
twice, eighteen months apart, because nobody could confirm it had already run and

54
00:03:01,585 --> 00:03:01,867
lost.

55
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:04,063
That's not a hypothetical inefficiency.

56
00:03:04,063 --> 00:03:08,794
It's the single most common way a testing budget gets wasted on established accounts

57
00:03:08,794 --> 00:03:10,371
that never built this habit.

58
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:15,342
Next lecture is where the numbers this record holds actually get read properly: at

59
00:03:15,342 --> 00:03:19,709
small spend, with real honesty about what a modest sample size can and can't tell

60
00:03:19,709 --> 00:03:19,924
you.

61
00:03:19,924 --> 00:03:24,291
Before that, go set up the record itself, even if it's just one column added to a

62
00:03:24,291 --> 00:03:25,855
spreadsheet you already have.

63
00:03:25,855 --> 00:03:30,330
Take whatever's currently running, name it properly using Section 5.4's convention,

64
00:03:30,330 --> 00:03:34,859
and add its first row: angle, date, and a blank where the result will eventually go.

65
00:03:34,859 --> 00:03:39,279
That single row is the start of a system that gets more valuable every single week

66
00:03:39,279 --> 00:03:40,358
you keep it running.

67
00:03:40,358 --> 00:03:44,455
Ask yourself, honestly, how many of your past tests you could still name and

68
00:03:44,455 --> 00:03:47,259
describe accurately today, off the top of your head.

69
00:03:47,259 --> 00:03:51,033
If the number is lower than you'd like, that's not a personal failing.

70
00:03:51,033 --> 00:03:55,346
It's exactly what this lecture predicted, and exactly what this system exists to

71
00:03:55,346 --> 00:03:59,713
fix, starting now rather than after another six months of untracked history piles

72
00:03:59,713 --> 00:03:59,875
up.
